WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:06.440
Disney's live-action Mulan is an epic action
film that's filled with tons of deep-cut references

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.330
to the original animation.

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:12.990
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm revealing 51 of the best Disney

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:17.260
easter eggs and hidden details to look out
for in the new movie, plus where to find the

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:20.500
secret tributes to Mushu.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.390
Some spoilers ahead, so take care if you haven't
seen the movie yet.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:28.070
The opening Disney logo has been transformed
with some cool new details appropriate to

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:29.070
Mulan.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:33.730
The iconic fairy tale castle has been given
some Chinese-style architectural additions;

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:37.850
and then just behind that is a long wall,
a reference to the opening scene of the animated

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:42.200
movie where the Huns began their invasion
by scaling the Great Wall of China.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:47.340
A shining phoenix also replaces the shooting
star that normally arcs over the castle.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:51.850
A nice touch given Mulan's connection with
the mythological beast in the new movie.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:56.570
The shapeshifting sorceress, played by Gong
Li, is a cool new character added to this

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:57.890
version of the story.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:02.260
And in her very first scene we get a hint
at how her arc will parallel Mulan's,

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:06.450
as she disguises herself by merging with the
body of a man on the Silk Road,

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:11.130
just like our heroine will later pretend to
be a man in order to join the Imperial army.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:16.380
The sorceress's name Xianniang as well as
her character arc are also hat-tips to a 17th-century

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.130
Chinese novel in which a princess of the same
name captured Mulan,

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:24.549
but then they became bonded sisters when Mulan
revealed her true identity to her.

00:01:24.549 --> 00:01:28.380
In Disney's movie, although Mulan and the
sorceress start out as enemies, they gain

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.889
respect for one another and ultimately become
allies.

00:01:31.889 --> 00:01:35.899
Although the sorceress can shapeshift into
various forms, she frequently turns into a

00:01:35.899 --> 00:01:40.529
hawk and so resembles Shan Yu's falcon side-kick
from the animated movie.

00:01:40.529 --> 00:01:45.359
And there are loads of incredible hawk-like
details on her costume, from her long feather-like

00:01:45.359 --> 00:01:46.359
skirt.

00:01:46.359 --> 00:01:50.319
to her belt made from bones;
and then there's her fearsome-looking hawk

00:01:50.319 --> 00:01:51.319
crown.

00:01:51.319 --> 00:01:55.020
She also has talon-shaped daggers that she
can toss with deadly force;

00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:00.809
and she can even grow bird-like claws on her
fingers that she uses to slash at her enemies.

00:02:00.809 --> 00:02:03.709
"I could tear you to pieces before you blink."

00:02:03.709 --> 00:02:06.329
Plus, according to the film's director Niki
Caro,

00:02:06.329 --> 00:02:09.879
her "makeup [is] hooded like a hawk's in the
form of a white mask,"

00:02:09.879 --> 00:02:13.019
with the use of the colour white being significant
because in China

00:02:13.019 --> 00:02:16.470
"white [is] the color of death [and] also
the color of purity".

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:21.709
Ming-Na Wen, the voice of the animated Mulan,
makes an awesome cameo to the tune of Reflection

00:02:21.709 --> 00:02:24.909
as she presents her live-action counterpart
to the emperor:

00:02:24.909 --> 00:02:26.310
"Your imperial highness.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.010
Hua Mulan."

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:32.310
Ming-Na Wen's official credit in the movie
is "Esteemed Guest" which is a lovely way

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:36.819
to acknowledge the legacy of the original
Disney film that the new movie builds upon.

00:02:36.819 --> 00:02:41.049
If you look at the train of her dress really
closely you can spot a phoenix, another nice

00:02:41.049 --> 00:02:44.260
detail connecting both Disney Mulans together.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:48.470
More on the other hidden phoenixes and their
true meaning in the movie later.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:52.909
And the green and red colours of Ming Na Wen's
ceremonial dress also appear to be a nod to

00:02:52.909 --> 00:02:56.849
the colours of the outfit that the animated
Mulan wore in front of the emperor,

00:02:56.849 --> 00:03:01.079
as well as the various shades of green she
wore during the animation.

00:03:01.079 --> 00:03:05.480
Everyone's favourite animated talking dragon
Mushu may not have made it in to the live-action

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.730
movie;
but the filmmakers still wanted to honour

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:10.379
him by including ancestral dragons in the
film,

00:03:10.379 --> 00:03:13.480
and you'll find them on various fabrics and
weapons.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.980
So watch the scene closely when Mulan's father
goes to sharpen his the family sword, because

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:22.900
the stone he uses is wrapped in a cloth with
an embroidered picture of a dragon.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:27.139
Also during Mulan's confrontation with the
sorceress, you can spot a dragon on the guard

00:03:27.139 --> 00:03:29.010
of Mulan's ancestral sword.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:32.879
And then there's a dragon on the hilt of the
sword that's gifted to Mulan by the emperor

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:34.730
at the end of the movie.

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:39.019
There's also a sneaky nod to one of Mushu's
most memorable lines from the animated movie

00:03:39.019 --> 00:03:43.079
when the garrison sergeant tells the troops
what will befall them if they are found being

00:03:43.079 --> 00:03:44.689
dishonest:
"Disgrace.

00:03:44.689 --> 00:03:45.969
Disgrace for you.

00:03:45.969 --> 00:03:47.290
Disgrace for your family.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:48.840
Disgrace for your village.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.370
Disgrace for your country."

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:51.370
"All right, that's it!

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:52.370
Dishonor!

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:53.370
Dishonor on your whole family.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:54.370
Make a note of this.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:56.060
Dishonor on you, dishonor on your cow.."

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:00.209
And Mulan's new mythological guardian, the
phoenix, is predominantly red, yellow and

00:04:00.209 --> 00:04:04.439
purple, a hat tip to Mushu's colours in the
90s classic.

00:04:04.439 --> 00:04:09.159
Another of Mulan's animated side-kicks was
Cri-Kee, the allegedly lucky cricket.

00:04:09.159 --> 00:04:11.610
Like Mushu, Cri-Kee doesn't appear in the
new movie;

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:15.689
however, there is a human character called
Cricket that joins the army at the same time

00:04:15.689 --> 00:04:16.689
as Mulan.

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:17.689
"I'm Cricket.

00:04:17.689 --> 00:04:19.190
My mother says that I was born under an auspicious
moon."

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:22.560
"Yep, this cricket's a lucky one!"

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.650
Cricket does seem to have good fortune.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:29.110
Despite all of the new recruits being terrible
archers, he's able to hit the target even

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:30.370
with his eyes closed.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:34.930
And he gets an awesome moment protecting Mulan
and clearing a path for her when he takes

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:38.940
out two Rouran soldiers with one lucky shot
from his bow.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:44.050
Like the animated Cri-Kee, he also miraculously
survives a potentially fatal accident during

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:45.290
the avalanche scene.

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:47.380
"Man, you are one lucky bug."

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:51.669
And again during the final showdown at the
Imperial City, to everyone's relief we discover

00:04:51.669 --> 00:04:56.280
Cricket really does enjoy good fortune just
like his animated counterpart also survived

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:57.410
the final battle.

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:00.280
"You are a lucky bug."

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:04.180
And fun fact: if you didn't already know,
crickets are viewed as symbols of good fortune

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:07.419
in China and were kept as pets from early
antiquity;

00:05:07.419 --> 00:05:12.060
however, Cri-Kee was so unpopular among many
of Disney Animation's filmmakers at the time

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:16.030
of the original film, that he almost ended
up scrapped from the movie.

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:20.509
The ever-so-memorable musical numbers from
the animation didn't make it into the live-action

00:05:20.509 --> 00:05:24.370
version, but there are still numerous references
to the iconic songs.

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:28.440
The popular "I'll Make A Man Out of You" is
referenced when the garrison's sergeant says:

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:32.169
"We're going to make men out of every single
one of you."

00:05:32.169 --> 00:05:36.020
That's followed by a training montage that
pays homage to the scene that accompanied

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.110
the song in the animation.

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:42.020
For example, when the recruits miserably fail
to get anywhere near the targets with their

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:44.919
bow and arrow, it's very similar to the original
film.

00:05:44.919 --> 00:05:49.090
They also have to carry heavy pails of water
up a mountain, similar to the weights they

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:51.289
were made to carry in the animation.

00:05:51.289 --> 00:05:54.760
In the new movie, this bucket challenge replaces
the pole climb,

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:58.849
and unlike the animated Mulan who struggled
carrying weights up the mountain,

00:05:58.849 --> 00:06:02.430
this new challenge is the one where she shines
and proves her worth.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:06.590
The interesting difference here is that it's
Mulan's chi that powers her up the mountain,

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:11.360
whereas in the animation it was her ingenuity
and determination in figuring out how to repurpose

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.340
the weights that helped her scale the log.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:19.020
At the heart of both the live-action and animated
Mulan movies is the idea of being true to

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:20.229
who you really are.

00:06:20.229 --> 00:06:25.050
The song "Reflection" is a core part of that
theme in the animation, and the new movie

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.960
pays tribute to that with an instrumental
version which plays at key moments.

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:33.930
So, when Mulan is summoned to her commanding
officer, as he begins to explain how "only

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:38.440
the most true connect [to their] chi", the
tune from "Reflection" is woven into the music

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:43.130
playing in the background.

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:47.419
The moment is not only a great easter egg,
but it also adds an extra emotional layer

00:06:47.419 --> 00:06:50.300
to the scene,
with "Reflection" continuing to play over

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:53.430
the montage of Mulan learning to reconnect
with her chi;

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:57.520
and finishing as she reaches the summit of
the mountain.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:04.300
And that montage also includes several visual
nods to when the animated Mulan sang the song

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:07.490
for the first time,
as the camera lingers over her reflection

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:11.220
in the water and also over a nearby magnolia
tree.

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:15.729
There's also a second musical reference during
this sequence with a shout-out to a line from

00:07:15.729 --> 00:07:18.180
"I'll Make A Man Out of You" when Commander
Tung says:

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:21.150
"Tranquil as a forest...
but on fire within."

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:25.909
You'll also hear "Reflection" during Mulan's
next big moment which comes just after her

00:07:25.909 --> 00:07:30.680
confrontation with the sorceress, when Hua
Jun dies and the true Mulan is born.

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:35.849
As she looks into her sword, she sees her
own reflection together with the word "true",

00:07:35.849 --> 00:07:40.220
and then the instrumental theme strikes up
again as she sheds her false identity and

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:44.919
reveals her true self.

00:07:44.919 --> 00:07:48.770
The theme of "Reflection" and being true to
yourself is even carried into the very final

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:52.040
scene of the new movie
when Mulan looks into the blade of her new

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:55.699
sword and sees herself and the phoenix reflected
back at her.

00:07:55.699 --> 00:08:00.229
This is in sharp contrast to the start of
the film just before she meets the matchmaker

00:08:00.229 --> 00:08:04.849
when she looks in the mirror and sees a version
of herself that doesn't reflect who she really

00:08:04.849 --> 00:08:05.849
is.

00:08:05.849 --> 00:08:10.009
Another shout-out to the animated movie comes
at the garrison when Ling looks at a scroll

00:08:10.009 --> 00:08:14.969
of a girl, which recalls how the song "A Girl
Worth Fighting For" started off in the animation.

00:08:14.969 --> 00:08:18.610
"That's what I said.

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:19.610
A girl worth fighting for."

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:23.040
And when the men start discussing who their
ideal woman would be, Po says

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:24.860
"I don't care what she looks like."

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:25.860
"I agree."

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:27.300
"I care what she cooks like."

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:29.970
Very similar to what was said in the animated
movie.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:34.410
I couldn't care less what she'll wear or what
she looks like.

00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:37.200
It all depends on what she cooks like."

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.440
The embarrassing moment for Mulan when she
enters the tent and Po drops his towel

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:46.080
is a humorous call-back to the animation's
lake scene when Mulan had to avert her eyes

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:48.860
after Yao climbed onto a rock stark naked.

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:49.870
"I am Yao!

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:53.480
King of the rock!

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:56.580
And there's nothin' you girls can do about
it."

00:08:56.580 --> 00:09:01.010
And there's a short instrumental version of
the song "Honor To Us All" when Mulan is primped

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:07.070
and polished to go to the matchmaker, in tribute
to the original film.

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:11.750
The magnolia tree by the lake in the live-action
movie and the blossoms that the camera focuses

00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:15.380
on when Mulan goes to bathe
are a sweet call-back to the magnolia tree

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:19.870
from the animated film and a crucial moment
in that story when Mulan's father told her

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:21.200
"This one's late.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:27.810
But I'll bet that when it blooms,
it will be the most beautiful of all."

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:32.370
Mulan's name actually means magnolia in Chinese
which is why the flower is so thematically

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:34.390
important in both the Disney movies.

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:39.310
For example, the film's costume designer added
a large embroidered magnolia blossom to the

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:43.950
centre of the undergarment Mulan wears as
part of her matchmaker outfit.

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:47.850
And when she practises her sword work and
learns to connect with her chi, she does so

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:50.240
near the magnolia tree by the lake.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:55.850
Mulan's matchmaker outfit also has embroidered
images of the phoenix which are a nice nod

00:09:55.850 --> 00:10:00.230
to the crucial role the mythological beast
plays for our heroine in this version of the

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:01.230
story.

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:05.670
The choice to use a phoenix as Mulan's guardian
in the new movie is especially interesting

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:09.630
because, as we see in the emperor's throne
room, the phoenix sits to his right while

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:11.260
the dragon sits to his left.

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:16.610
And, of course, Mulan's journey takes her
to the Imperial City where she saves his life.

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:21.130
In China, the phoenix has often been viewed
as a symbol of the empress or a female member

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:24.490
of the royal family while the dragon represented
the emperor,

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:29.130
with the two together representing a harmonious
and balanced relationship.

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:34.000
In addition to that, in Chinese mythology
the phoenix was originally two separate birds,

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:38.060
one male and one female, though they later
merged to become one.

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:42.280
And so the duality of the phoenix makes it
a perfect guardian and symbol for Mulan who

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:46.260
as a woman takes on what's perceived to be
a traditionally male role in the story.

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:50.810
And there are also so many call-backs and
little twists on moments from the original

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:51.810
movie.

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:55.780
One of my favourites is when Mulan returns
to the army to deliver news about Böri Khan's

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:58.680
secret plan to attack the Imperial City.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:02.580
Commander Tung says it's foolish to listen
to her given she was dishonest about who she

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:05.960
really was, to which Honghui replies:
"You would believe Hua Jun,

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:07.680
why do you not believe Hua Mulan?"

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:11.310
It's a line that echoes what Mulan said in
her own defence in the animated movie.

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:13.250
"You said you'd trust Ping.

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:14.910
Why is Mulan any different?"

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:19.480
Then when she faces off against Böri Khan
without a sword and uses her skills and smarts

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:23.070
to take it from him,
it's a call-back to how she used her quick-thinking

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:27.020
to wrangle Shan Yu's sword from him defeating
him in the animation.

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:31.690
"Looks like you're all out of ideas."

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:32.830
"Not quite."

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:36.880
And the single soldier who survives Böri
Khan's attack at the beginning of the film

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:39.630
and delivers the news of the defeat to the
Imperial City

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:44.360
"This soldier is the only survivor."
is a throw-back to how Shan Yu decided only

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:47.010
one man was needed to send his message to
the emperor.

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:49.610
"How many men does it take to deliver a message?"

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:50.610
"One."

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:55.960
As well as Disney easter eggs, the new movie
also includes a few shout-outs to the original

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:58.020
Ballad of Mulan that the story is based on.

00:11:58.020 --> 00:12:02.520
When the adult Mulan arrives home at the beginning
of the film, she tells her parents about two

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:05.260
rabbits that were running alongside her while
she was out riding:

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:08.200
"I think one was a male, one was a female.

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:11.380
But you know you can't really tell when they
are running that fast."

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:15.310
Mulan's comment isn't just a reference to
the theme of gender roles in the story;

00:12:15.310 --> 00:12:18.950
it's also a nice hat-tip to the original ballad
which ends with the lines:

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:23.880
"Two rabbits running close to the ground,
How can they tell if I am male or female?"

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:27.821
And when we see Mulan's little sister busy
at the loom at the start of the film, it's

00:12:27.821 --> 00:12:32.510
a nod to how the original poem starts off
with Mulan herself weaving at a loom.

00:12:32.510 --> 00:12:37.330
I really liked some of the bold creative choices
in the new movie and in my other Mulan video

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:42.630
I do a deep dive into the best and worst differences
in the live-action film compared to Disney's

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:43.630
animation.

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:46.330
Tap here to watch that next or follow the
link in the video description.

00:12:46.330 --> 00:12:51.230
So, did you spot any other easter eggs or
interesting details in the new movie?

00:12:51.230 --> 00:12:53.300
And what were your favourite moments?

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:54.800
Comment with your thoughts below.

00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:57.550
If you enjoyed this don't forget to leave
a thumbs-up.

00:12:57.550 --> 00:13:00.550
It really helps and I hugely appreciate it.

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:05.180
Tap left for my full Mulan and Disney live-action
playlist or tap right for something else you're

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:06.180
sure to like.

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:08.290
Thanks for watching and see ya next time.

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:09.180
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

